--- body ---
## Context

When a raid finishes and the worktree is still active, the user has no
way to tell from the transcript which git context `erg` is operating in.
Last bump: user ran `erg ready` assuming they were back on main, but the
session was still inside a worktree — so the ready output reflected the
worktree's ticket state, not main's.

## Design

`resolveDir()` logs one line to stderr after successful resolution:

    erg: branch <name> (tickets/)

where `<name>` is the output of `git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD` run
from the resolved store directory. This is a log message, not a print —
a function logging its own operation is a common and accepted pattern.

Stderr is the correct channel: stdout must stay parseable for skills and
agents that pipe `erg list --json`, `erg ready`, etc.

Degrades silently when git is unavailable (offline contract): if the git
command fails, emit no banner.

## Scope

All commands that call `resolveDir()` get the banner automatically:
`list`, `ready`, `check`, `next-id`, `new`, `close`, `log`, `label`,
`unlabel`, `archive`, `rm`, `migrate`.

Commands that do not call `resolveDir()` are unaffected:
`validate`, `version`, `update`, `init`.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `resolveDir()` prints `erg: branch <name> (<relpath>)\n` to stderr
      after successful resolution
- [ ] Relative path used when store is under cwd; absolute otherwise
- [ ] No output when git is unavailable (offline-safe)
- [ ] `erg list`, `erg ready`, `erg close` all show the banner in tests
- [ ] `--json` output is unaffected (banner is on stderr only)
- [ ] `make check` passes for code introduced by this PR (pre-existing failures tracked in 0195 are out of scope)
